WebKit: the UI process can see what an update scrolled and repainted, and scrolls its backing store in place.

The classic drawing area reports any update that scrolled as "the whole view needs display", so
a client that paints from the backing store (WKPagePaint) copies, converts and hands on every
pixel of the view for each step of a scroll, although the web process painted only the rows the
scroll exposed. `WKPageSetUpdateCallback` gives the client what the update said instead: the
rectangle that moved, by how much, and the rectangles painted afterwards. With a callback set the
view is not reported as needing display for those updates (the callback is the notice).

`BackingStore::scroll` (Skia) read the moved area into a freshly allocated bitmap and wrote it
back: a multi-megabyte allocation, its page faults and two copies for every step of a scroll. A
raster surface's pixels are now moved where they are (rows in the order that never reads a row
already written), with the old copy kept for a surface that cannot be peeked.

